</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">REAL-WORLD EXPERIMENTS</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We set up real-world experiments based on the Galaxea R1 Lite, a dual-arm mobile platform. The system consists of three omnidirectional wheels, two 6-DoF arms, two wrist cameras, and a head camera, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV-C Ablation Study ‣ IV SIMULATION EXPERIMENTS ‣ AtomVLA: Scalable Post-Training for Robotic Manipulation via Predictive Latent World Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. In our experiments, the mobile module is kept stationary. We categorize our real-world tasks into two difficulty levels: <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard</span>.
<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Basic</span> tasks are short-horizon and involve basic manipulation actions; they include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stack bowls</span>: Pick the bowl and put it on the other.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Put fruit into basket</span>: Pick the fruit and place it into the basket.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hang cup:</span> Hang the cup on the mug rack.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Open drawer:</span> Switch the drawer from closed to open.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard</span> tasks target deformable-object manipulation and long-horizon, and they include:</p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fold T-shirt:</span> Fold a T-shirt into a target folded configuration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fold towel:</span> Fold the towel twice to reach the target configuration.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting.</span> To comprehensively assess the real-world performance of AtomVLA, we conduct evaluations under two different settings: Standard <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ST)</span> and Generalization <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(GE)</span>. In the Standard Setting, tasks are executed under conditions consistent with the training demonstrations to verify the foundational manipulation capabilities of the learned policy. To rigorously evaluate the generalization capabilities of our framework in unstructured physical environments, the Generalization Setting introduces four distinct types of perturbations during deployment, as shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ V-B Results Analysis ‣ V REAL-WORLD EXPERIMENTS ‣ AtomVLA: Scalable Post-Training for Robotic Manipulation via Predictive Latent World Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object Position Variation</span>: The initial spatial coordinates of both target objects and receptacles are randomized within the workspace.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unseen Distractor Objects</span>: Novel items, absent from the training demonstrations, are introduced in the vicinity of the target objects.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Height Variation</span>: Receptacles are elevated to varying heights using arbitrary support structures.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction Variation</span>: Task instructions are replaced with diverse, semantically equivalent expressions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Details.</span> Each task comprises 100 demonstrations. The model undergoes SFT on the real-world dataset for 2 epochs, followed by post-training, where we sample 100K trajectories with 10 candidates per state. During the evaluation phase, we conduct 20 trials per task. Specifically, under the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GE</span> setting, the environmental conditions for these 20 trials are randomly distributed across the four types to rigorously assess robustness. Detailed hyperparameters are provided in the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key Finding 6: AtomVLA  demonstrates superior robustness and generalization in real-world robotic tasks.</span> While AtomVLA  and the baseline <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> exhibit comparable foundational capabilities under the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ST</span> setting, the critical advantage of our approach emerges under the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GE</span> setting. When subjected to spatial, visual, and instruction variations, the baseline performance degrades sharply to an average success rate of 29.2%. Conversely, AtomVLA  demonstrates substantial resilience by maintaining a 47.5% average success rate, yielding an absolute improvement of 18.3% over <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. This enhanced robustness is particularly pronounced in <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard</span> tasks involving deformable objects, which require precise long-horizon control where execution errors easily compound. For instance, under the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GE</span> setting for the Fold T-shirt and Fold towel tasks, AtomVLA  sustains success rates of 25% and 35% respectively, far exceeding the baseline. These results validate that leveraging subtask instruction decomposition and world-model-guided post-training effectively mitigates error accumulation even in highly unstructured environments.</p>
</div>
